yay~
absolutely, and it always makes me feel good to contribute *something* of value
:)
I am planning to be at TPAC so that'll be good
awesome
another question
relates to font-weights
if you have three nested spans
okay, I was around for part of that discussion earlier g/a
with the font weights 'bolder', 'bolder', and 'lighter'
but you only have two weights, normal and bold
is the innermost span bold or normal?
what's the rule look like?
(A related question: if given two nested spans 'bolder' and 'lighter', should the inner span always be the same weight as outside the spans?)
the rule looks like
&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder&quot;&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder&quot;&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: lighter&quot;&gt; Am I normal or bold?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;
well, I might argue that the only span of relevance is that directly spanning the content, and therefore, in this case, &quot;lighter&quot;
all of the other spans are empty
oh, well they could have text in them :)
or they could be &lt;div&gt;s
also, what's the parent? It seems that in part this would be inheritance no?
the behavior would have to be the same
no, there's no inheritance
it's just &quot;bolder&quot; is a relative term
if you have a font with three weights
then the innermost span would be the same weight as the outermost span, bolder than the text outside the spans
but lighter than the text inside the middle span
normal weight &lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder&quot;&gt; bold weight &lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder&quot;&gt; extra-bold weight &lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: lighter&quot;&gt; bold weight&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;
so it's relative to the immediate parent span?
like that
yep
urgh
the question is, what happens if you only have two weights
I say it goes to normal
in some ways it feels like as the designer
I'd need a &quot;clue&quot; to say, okay, there's a breakdown in the relation here
if the author is intending to have a lighter color
right, well
that should be explicit (I hate hate hate relative values btw)
for this very reason
it's defined as lighter than the parent
but the designer might be trying to get back to the middle value here
if there were multiple weights (like 3 or 5) that's what happens
I don't know
it's confusing.
I don't know either."")
