CONVERSATION:
lsmith: sleeping bag in the office... thats the spirit! :)
ap ap ap, &quot;Rebuild if 5% of the rows have changed&quot;... not true for oracle ...
why do people love to rebuild indexes..
hali :)
there is a very asktom thread about index rebuilding and someone referes to an article by donald burelson ... tom responds with &quot;yes, very pretty graphs indeed. he likes graphs, but not facts&quot;
unless you do really really massive dml you almost never need to rebuild an index..
the index becomes better and faster unless something redical happens... oracle collects more and more stats about it
hali: similar advice can be found in the oracle sql tuning pocket reference by oreilly
although the author refrains from giving an exact percentage
burleson claims to be an expert... tom responds
I'm sure DB is an &quot;expert&quot; based on the definition:
- experts CLAIM things
- scientists PROVE things
:)
*smack*
found the thread
<a href="http://asktom.oracle.com/pls/ask/f?p=4950">http://asktom.oracle.com/pls/ask/f?p=4950</a>:8:3521033655157586064::NO::F4950_P8_DISPLAYID,F4950_P8_CRITERIA:2913600659112
worth a quick browse
spitzenklasse
anyways .. if you are talking about a table where you delete as many rows as you insert
then it will probably all run even in the long run
however if you have a table that has very different amount of data in different situations it can be tricky
indeed
then partitioning could be fun
then again .. the question if you are then really hurting from getting the same speed when the table is close to empty
as you did when it was full
speaking of that...
have you tried the partitioning in mysql 5.1 yet?
however if you have spare cpu cycles as night .. you can make sure you do not get unlucky during the day :)
hali: i think i tried it once .. really early .. but all i did was a create and 1 alter ..
ok
the syntax even looks ok :)
its definately much further along feature-wise than pgsql
yes
the pgsql model is sucky
and its very limited
8.2 will be better
ah .. last time i checked there was nothing decidedly on the horizon yet
its kind of unfortunate that this open source db list hasnt really taken off
i was hoping that it could lead to some standardization efforts for non ANSI SQL features
for all open source databases
but the camps are too entrenched
anyways .. the pocket reference i mentioned showed that if you delete all the rows an index matches, the read time remains almost unchanged
which is unsuprising giving how things are implemented
did you know that maxdb requires no maintaince at all? .. in a way you could say its just less &quot;optimized&quot; ..
they do all balancing, deleting etc immediatly
so they get constant speed
which they claim is a big plus for 24/7 shops
hm, for good and bad...
they also do not havy mvcc IIRC
i have faint recollections that they are working on that though
anyways .. i am a bit pissed at mysql ab .. once again they came to me with a job
but then came up with some excuse once i have sat through interviews
saying again its not me ..
its just that things internally changed
gna
bastards
yeah
time to call larry? :)
maybe :)
i know the oracle guys read my blog ..
so do the ibm guys
so do the postgresql guys
maybe i should auction myself off on ebay :)
that could get some strange effects
:)
some nuke building company in iran is the highest bidder :D
hehe
you dont know how funny that was
my name at birth was &quot;kahwe haddad&quot;
because my biological father is from iran :)
but he left when i was 2 .. and i was adopted by an american when my mother married again
which turned my name into &quot;lukas kahwe haddad&quot;."")
