CONVERSATION:
does RMAN support timing?
huh?
like sqlplus's 'set timing on' to report execution time
no, why should it?
so I would have a better idea of how long a duplicate or backup command took
why shouldn't it, when sqlplus does
RMAN isn't sqlplus, is it
you have yet to provide any real reason
I'm quite well aware that it isn't
it isn't WRITTEN that way
christ almighty you are smart
look, jerk
it executes commands
if you want timing
and you're on UNIX/Linux:
time rman
yes I can use the bash 'time' command with a script
so why don't you?
which I use for my backups
but I don't have a satisfactory script for duplication yet
there IS no 'timing' option for RMAN
so I'm doing it manually
THANK YOU
that answers the original question
which you HAD several MINUTES ago
had you read the response properly
You seem to think that it would be stupid to do so though, which I will debate to the death
go ahead
debate, I see no purpose for it
Tell me why you think it is such a ridiculous idea
I gave you precise purposes
Why, then, provide timing for sqlplus commands?
users could just as easily run bash 'time'
why should RMAN be bloated to include such 'functionality' when it's designed to perform backup and recovery
and backup and recovery are complex enough issues with a  live Oracle database
In this *particular* case, I think providing the mechanism to measure timing would be worth the 'bloat'
my opinion, of course
you're in the minority
I'd love to see that poll
you're the only person I've known in several years to ask for it
but I imagine that if DBAs were asked if they would consider such a function useful, they just might answer yes, even if they hadn't considered that question before and/or publicly asked for it
that's your guess
which is all we've got, I'm afraid
so here's another RMAN question
keep guessing
I'm trying to dupe from remote prod to local aux, but when I give the &quot;duplicate target database to dev1 nofilenamecheck;&quot; command, it just says that it can't find backup or copy of any of the target datafiles
but when I connect via rman to the remote prod instance, I can list backup of one of the specified datafiles
which means you either didn't copy them to the the destination machine or haven't used an nfs mount to that machine from the remote
you can't keep the files on the remote server and expect them to be read on the local macnine
you are indeed correct, sir.
RMAN is validating against the remote
so RMAN doesn't do file transfer
of COURSE it can 'see' the backup pieces
no, and the documentation CLEARLY states this
if you have a link, let me know.
tahiti.oracle.com
choose your release, search for duplicate database
you'll find the information in the RMAN manuals
&quot;All backups and archived redo logs used for creating and recovering the duplicate database, however, must be accessible by the server session on the duplicate host. If the duplicate host is not the same as the target host, then you must make backups on disk on the target host available to the duplicate host with the same full path name as in the primary database.&quot;
gracias
either by nfs mount or by direct copy
right
my apologies for disappointing you for not being stupid
I imagine that NFS mount can just be read-only on the aux host
I never said you were stupid
implied, then
I was just upset that you thought the very notion of a timing command was ridiculous
gee, EXCUSE ME for not agreeing with your decision to bloat RMAN
you are free to disagree
oh, gee, that's big of you
I think your use of the term 'bloat' is a bit liberal in this case
it's just timing
I think it isn't
1. store timestamp at start
you have no idea what it takes to write such a timing routine
2. store timestamp at end
3. subtract millis
4. convert to human-readable
I have no idea how Oracle might choose to implement, and I'm not sure I'd like to be the one to suggest a change. The timing routine is designed to be used by the RMAN command 'time'. The command is designed to be used by the RMAN command 'time'. The command is designed to be used by the RMAN command 'time'. The command is designed to be used by the RMAN command 'time'. The command is designed to be used by the RMAN command 'time'. The command is designed to be used by the RMAN command 'time'. The command is designed to be used by the RMAN command 'time'. The command is designed to be used"")
